# inv-patch: 30-l10-pf
# purpose: L10 software prefetch in the Mode D lever completion loops (spec I3 follow-up). The I3 profile of the Mode D completion path on a 1-core cell showed a dependent miss chain: device-written 32 B record in the host ring -> tr = &pqpair->tr[cid] (nvme_tracker is 4096 B, so every tracker header maps to one L1 set) -> tr->req -> callback -> nvme_free_request(req) (touches req->stailq and req->qpair, offsets 0x90/0x98). Stock upstream SPDK hides the equivalent misses in the mode-0 CQ loop (nvme_pcie_qpair_process_completions) with two software prefetches: (a) when the NEXT CQE is already valid, __builtin_prefetch(&pqpair->tr[next_cpl->cid]); (b) before completing the current tracker, __builtin_prefetch(&tr->req->stailq). The Mode D loops have neither. This overlay adds them and measures how much of the IAU completion cost a plain prefetch recovers. Env SPDK_INV_PF=1 (default off, read once at load by inv_env_prime()). New tail byte inv_pf (after inv_db_n; NOT an inv_flags bit, all eight are taken, and a bit would divert the stock poll). inv_process_completions branches once per poll on inv_pf to a separate instantiation of the always_inline loops with a const pf parameter (existing call sites pass false). pf in inv_records_tr (mirrors upstream): after the pop, peek the next record's phase word w3 at the already-advanced cq_rec_head/cq_rec_phase (volatile load, same bit test as inv_rec_pop, wrap already handled by the pop); if valid, prefetch &pqpair->tr[next_cid]; then before the callback prefetch &req->stailq. pf in inv_records_trfree: the same peek reads two words of the next record, w3 (phase) and, if valid, w0 (the next req pointer), and prefetches only &next_req->stailq (cb_fn, cb_arg, stailq and qpair share that line in the lean layout; line 0 is only used by the next allocation's memset, so it is left alone as upstream does); no extra prefetch before the current callback, req came from the record. Both loops guard the peek with words == 4. The peek never consumes or modifies ring state. Set at the end of IO qpair construct, on Mode D (not Mode E, not mode 0) IO qpairs only, and only when the final inv_flags routes completions to inv_records_tr or inv_records_trfree with words == 4 (TRFREE without REC16/REC64, or any of REC32/DESC/ADDR without REC16/REC64 and without THIN; loop=trfree iff the TRFREE bit survives, else tr). Otherwise inv_pf stays 0 and one process-wide line "INV_PF ignored: needs a 32 B lever completion loop (set IAU_INV_REC_BYTES=32 or SPDK_INV_TRFREE=1)" is printed, only for Mode D IO qpairs that have a record ring (silent in mode 0 and Mode E). SPDK_INV_PF alone sets no inv_flags bit, so the stock paths run and the knob is ignored. Matched controls: IAU_INV_REC_BYTES=32;SPDK_INV_PF=1 versus IAU_INV_REC_BYTES=32 (tracker loop), SPDK_INV_TRFREE=1;SPDK_INV_PF=1 versus SPDK_INV_TRFREE=1 (trfree loop), IAU_INV_DESC_BYTES=40;SPDK_INV_PF=1 versus IAU_INV_DESC_BYTES=40. Composes with 25 and 27 (they pick the tracker loop at 32 B records) and with 28. Guard (also in this patch): at the end of IO qpair construct every IO qpair whose final inv_flags is nonzero prints "INV_FLAGS qid=N flags=0x%02x" whichever lever set the bits; check_l.py FAILs a point whose lines differ from the flags computed from the lever envs (an unexpected bit would divert the stock paths, the 25 unset-env bug). Build note: with the knob unset the stock nvme_pcie_qpair_submit_request and nvme_pcie_qpair_process_completions are instruction-identical to the same chain without 30 except __LINE__ immediates, but inv_process_completions is re-laid-out by this patch, so every lever arm and its control must come from the same (rebuilt) inv-levers binary. The PF delta includes the early read of the next record line while the device may still be writing it (the same as upstream's next-CQE peek).
# safety: SAFE (prefetch hints only; no queue, record, tracker or ring state changes, and a prefetch of a bad address cannot fault)
# breaks: none (a peek reads ring words that the next pop would read anyway; the extra prefetches can only change timing; the INV_FLAGS line is evidence only). Only the 32 B loops are covered, so 16 B and 64 B records and the thin loops ignore the knob with the INV_PF ignored line. The trfree arm prefetches a req pointer taken from a device-written record, which a corrupt record could make arbitrary, but a prefetch never faults. §4 L evidence: host "INV_PF qid=N loop=tr|trfree" per Mode D IO qpair at construct when the knob took effect (no destroy line, the knob changes no state), and "INV_FLAGS qid=N flags=0x%02x" per IO qpair with nonzero inv_flags; check_l.py expects INV_PF when SPDK_INV_PF=1, mode 4, not THIN, REC bytes not 16/64 and (REC bytes 32, TRFREE, ADDR va|vtophys or DESC bytes 32|40|64), and FAILs unless the INV_FLAGS lines match the flags computed from the lever envs exactly (none when no bit is expected, else one per IO qpair with that value)
# env: SPDK_INV_PF=1 (default 0; takes effect only on top of a 32 B lever completion loop: IAU_INV_REC_BYTES=32, SPDK_INV_TRFREE=1, SPDK_INV_ADDR=va|vtophys or IAU_INV_DESC_BYTES=32|40|64; alone it is ignored)
# base: 00-spin.diff,01-floor.diff,10-walk.diff,11-deadstores.diff,12-residue.diff,13-iport.diff,14-stathist.diff,20-l1-trfree.diff,22-l2-thin.diff,23-l3-rec.diff,24-l4-idleflush.diff,25-l6-desc.diff,26-l7-app.diff,27-l9-addr.diff,28-s4-dbspin.diff
